[Music]  [Music]  hey folks my name is derek fisher and i  want to walk through a little bit of  threat modeling we're going to use uh  owasp's threat dragon to do a threat  model there's other tools out there that  exist one of the other more popular ones  is the microsoft threat model tool  but uh obasp threat dragon's pretty  pretty good one too it's a little more  intuitive and gives you a little more  freedom but either one's fine but in  this case we're going to use  threat dragon when we create a threat  model the first thing we're trying to  understand is what does the system look  like what are the different components  that are part of that system the  different interactions the different  boundaries  and the whole goal there is to identify  threats that exist within that system so  if you have a basic web application  with a database and external access  across the internet  then there's a lot of different threats  that are posed to that system so  creating a threat model will allow you  to identify what those threats are and  then put together a  mitigation or remediation strategy to  try to address those threats  the last part of that too is to validate  that the mitigation remediation uh are  effective and that the threats are  actually real so in some cases you might  identify threats that just don't apply  so the first thing you want to do is  create a diagram and you want to start  out with a large white board if you're  doing this in a group setting and what  you want to do is draw out the  architecture now if you're doing this by  yourself you can just go right into the  tool and build out the diagram within  the tool you can do it in powerpoint you  could do it in vizio you could do it in  any kind of drawing application as well  if you don't have a whiteboard  the diagram should be as in-depth as  possible but you don't want to create an  eye chart you don't want to make  something that's  so  large that you know it's unwieldy and  you're not able to actually get anywhere  with it you know one of the rules of  thumb that i try to use is that if i  don't have  or if the organization doesn't have  control over how to mitigate a threat  with that given part of the system then  it doesn't necessarily need to be in  scope maybe the interaction has to be  there so for instance maybe you uh work  with a a third party that is calling  your api you can't control the code on  that third party uh therefore any  threats that exist at that third in that  third party for instance maybe that  third party uh has an exposure to  cross-site scripting or something like  that well you can't fix that so it  isn't in scope now  the interaction of that third party  calling your api is absolutely in scope  so hopefully that makes a little bit of  sense so some of the things you want to  consider obviously a scope you want to  make sure that you have  all the pieces that are part of the of  the system identified in the model you  want to identify the different actors  and the abusers that might interact with  your system you want to get all the  system components database uh web  application servers um  any kind of  load balancers and  different configuration files it might  be on file systems and so forth you want  to create you want to understand and  identify all the different processes and  data flows that are part of the system  and then any kind of backup or  monitoring or logging components that  are part of the system as well because  again all those things will will come  into play so one thing to keep in mind  about the the scoping though is that you  want to make sure that you're creating  this threat model which is a diagram and  it's basically an architecture diagram  so you want to boil down the workflows  to something uh as small as you can so  you don't want to create you know large  workflows unless of course it's you know  makes sense but the purpose of scoping  small is so that you can  work on a a  given  area  and actually get to the end of the  threat model if you have a large model  with a lot of different  components in it it's going to be  difficult for you to be able to uh  tackle that whole thing  you know like the common phrase of how  you eat an elephant  you do it one bite at a time so you want  to try to scope it as small as possible  so you can concentrate on small areas  get to a finished  threat threat model and then be able to  move on so the diagram must help you  understand and discuss you know the  system security considerations uh the  diagram should contain the items  determined in the scope and if you miss  something it's okay  threat models are living documents so  they're things that  you will be able to go back to and  update change as things as you learn  something new again we're going to use  owasp threat dragon and for this so  here's a mockup of a simple web  application  and you're going to see  a user and an admin and then that dotted  line is a boundary  they go through the web server which  then goes to the app server which then  goes to a database sorry that's a typo  there but it then goes to the database  but the important thing here is to draw  make sure you have boundaries that you  include  all the components of the system now  again this is a very very simple web  application obviously this is just for  demo purposes um a true threat model is  going to be much more robust than this  so now you have the diagram you want to  start looking at what can go wrong and  there's a couple different threat  modeling frameworks and so  one of the most popular ones or i  shouldn't say popular but one of the  more common ones is stride and stride  stands for spoofing tampering  repudiation information disclosure  denial service and elevation of  privileges  and so it classifies threats into those  uh those six categories so spoofing is  somebody being able to impersonate an  account tampering is  uh changing data so that it's not in the  original form it was in  repudiation is being able to tie back an  activity to a given  account or user information disclosure  is confidentiality breach denial service  is an availability breach and then  elevation of privileges is being able to  escalate or elevate a user's  privilege to a higher level in order to  perform additional tasks so let's take a  look at threat drag it's a pretty simple  tool again it's pretty intuitive but  what we're going to do here is uh  get started with a completely new empty  threat model just going to create a new  model  here and i'm gonna go ahead and just  call this  my web app  uh owner is me  reviewer is  jane doe  and system description we're just going  to call this um  [Music]  website for  cat pictures  okay  now i'm going to go ahead and add a new  diagram and just call this the web app  add it  save  all right so you see here that it's  going to create this diagram that you  can then open  and it's a blank canvas it's in stride  right now  meaning that's the way we're going to  categorize these vulnerabilities where  these threats will be fine  so first thing i'm going to do is put  down uh two actors i want to have a  basic user  and i'm going to have an admin uh that's  able to log into the site to make  changes  now put down two processes  going to make this the  web server  make this the app server  and i'm going to put down a store  which is going to be  the database  i'm going to put down a trust boundary  so obviously  the access is going to be over the  internet  okay  so what you want to do is because the  user is going to be accessing the web  server it's pretty simple to do this  here with threat dragon is you're able  to just click on the arrow here it's  going to say select another part of the  model  and there you go and then i'm going to  create another flow that goes between  the web server and the app server  another one that goes back to the  database  same thing here  and  there you go  that's your basic threat model now in  order to manage threats  first thing we can do is let's say we  want to look at the threats on the web  server we can add stride elements to  this and it's going to give you just  basically generic elevation of  privileges and you can see here it says  one of six so it's going to add all of  the stride category threats to this so  i'm not going to step through each one  of them here but  we're going to accept a basic or generic  elevation of privileges  we'll accept the basic spoofing  and so forth  you can click do this for remaining it's  going to add them all and so you'll see  here that elevation of privileges you  know spoofing tampering uh repudiation  information disclosure denial service  and elevation privileges so stride or  have all been added and you can take a  look at this and say yeah there's a  spoofing attack here on the web server  you can add additional description here  if you have it mitigated here we'll say  that we have  mfa  enabled  and  [Music]  strong authentication  and same mitigated save  and it's going to mark it as being  mitigated  now you could also add  your own threats let's say  the web server  um i want to add another threat so i can  say add new threat  let's say the um  admin  credentials i know this is kind of  contradictory to what i just put there  but credentials  are shared  and static  so it never changes it's shared  um in this case that would be a  spoofing um you could because there's no  accountability you could say a spoofing  you could say it's repudiation depending  on uh the case here but  if the credentials are shared and it  gets loose within the organization  you can say it's also possibly elevation  of privileges if it goes to  if a non-admin user is able to gain  access to that so i'm just gonna again  just for demo purposes here i'm just  gonna add this  and we could say here that uh mfa you  know again is is the mitigation um which  again i'm just showing this here as an  example these are all just uh samples  here but mfa enabled  so therefore it's mitigated  okay  and so you would be able to do this with  the remainder of your  the remainder of the threat model here  you can mark things out of scope um just  put in your  reason for being out of scope if you are  going to do that  there is some opportunity here to add  privilege levels and things like that  the store  has additional components that you're  able to  add to reduce reduced threats or show  that some of the threats might be  mitigated and so forth but again this is  just a simple way of doing a quick and  dirty threat model and be able to track  the mitigations that are there and so  with that i hope you enjoyed this little  walk through and  have a good day thank you

